   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( f(x) = \\ln x \\). The second-degree Taylor polynomial for \\( f \\) about \\( x = 1 \\) is used to approximate \\( f\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\). Find the Lagrange error bound for this approximation, taking \\( M \\) to be the maximum of \\( |f'''(x)| \\) on \\( \\left[\\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right] \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": [
         "Rational",
         1,
         3
      ]
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "A degree 2 polynomial needs the derivative of order 3: \\( f'''(x) = \\frac{2}{x^{3}} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            2,
            [
               "Power",
               "x",
               3
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Its absolute value is monotonic on \\( \\left[\\frac{1}{2}, 1\\right] \\), so the maximum is at an endpoint: \\( M = 16 \\). The bound is \\( \\frac{M\\,|x - 1|^{3}}{3!} \\).",
         "mathjson": 16
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "With \\( |x - 1| = \\frac{1}{2} \\), the bound is \\( \\frac{16 \\cdot \\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{3}}{6} = \\frac{1}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            3
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "The error is at most \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Rational",
            1,
            3
         ]
      }
   ],
